<title>THAILAND: Union Asia says most loans hedged.</title>
<headline>Union Asia says most loans hedged.</headline>
<dateline>BANGKOK 1997-07-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Thailand's Union Asia Finance and Securities Plc said on Tuesday that the impact of the baht's float was minimal because most of its foreign loans were hedged.</p>
<p>In a filing to the Stock Exchange of Thailand, Union Asia said its foreign loan exposure was about $199 million of which $192 million had been hedged against foreign exchange risk.</p>
<p>Thai authorities last week floated the baht after pegging it for 13 years to a basket of currencies dominated by the U.S. dollar.</p>
<p>The move, seen as de facto devaluation, drove the baht down by around 15-20 percent against the dollar.</p>
<p>At 0825 GMT on Tuesday, the baht was trading at 29.050 to the dollar from 24.25 just before the managed float was announded.</p>
<p>-- Bangkok newsroom (662) 652-0642</p>
</text>
